00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.269 align:start position:0%
for decades scientists have been digging  up dirt about the surface of mars but  what do we know about its atmosphere  now scientists have probed the upper  atmosphere of the red planet to discover  what it's made of and why mars lost most  of it to space  the martian atmosphere is rife with  carbon dioxide with smaller amounts of  nitrogen and argon  studying mars's atmosphere could reveal  the history of its climate and help  answer a vexing question was mars ever  habitable  it could also provide clues for  understanding our own planet  since 2014 nasa's maven spacecraft has  been studying mars atmosphere orbiting  the planet a whopping thousand plus  times in four years  previous probes have discovered all  sorts of things about mars's lower  atmosphere the mars reconnaissance  orbiter gauge the temperature water and  ozone concentrations  and the exomars trace gas orbiter scoped  out gases like methane which could be  evidence for biological and geological  activity  but a crucial piece of the puzzle was  missing  data from a region of the upper  atmosphere known as the thermosphere the  latest study used maven to measure wind  patterns roughly 140 to 240 kilometers  above the planet's surface  over one and a half mars years roughly  687 earth days the spacecraft repeatedly  dipped from space down into mars  thermosphere  as if using a butterfly net it captured  molecules and analyzed them with mass  spectrometry allowing scientists to  measure the wind velocities  the data showed that wind in mars's  thermosphere circulates in simpler ways  than on earth in patterns that persist  over multiple seasons  giant atmospheric waves are generated by  large variations in the height of mars's  surface far below  these results bring scientists a few  steps closer to solving the riddle of  mars upper atmosphere shedding light on  its makeup and contributing to big  objectives like the search for habitable  planets and the delivery of a complete  picture of earth's climate  just maybe that could remind people back  on earth why we look to the sky for  answers  [Music]  you
 

